Q: Assigning multiple assets to multiple variables on inspector A: <p>You should create a public List of Audio Sources which will appear in inspector. Lock the inspector by clicking look in right top.
Now select all the files from Project and drag on list. All files will be added to list. Now if you want a specific file you can find the file by its name for example:</p>

<pre><code>AudioSource GetAudioSource(string SourceName)
{
return audioSourceList.Find(item =&gt; item.Name == SourceName);
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I check if a returned value is an integer in Java and C? A: <p>Let's go with Java, which is the only one I can talk about.
Suppose we have code like the following:</p>
<pre><code>boolean returnNumber = true;

public Object getValue() {
   if( returnNumber ) return 1;
   return “1”;
}
</code></pre>
<p>To operate with the value received from <strong>getValue()</strong>, you can use the following:</p>
<pre><code>void myMethod() {
   Object aux = getValue();
   if( aux.getClass().equals( Integer.class ) ) callIntegerMethod( (int) aux );
   if( aux.getClass().equals( String.class ) ) callStringMethod( (String) aux );
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can also use:</p>
<pre><code>if( aux instanceof Integer value ) {
</code></pre>
<p>This way, we check and obtain a variable with the corresponding casting.</p>

Q: How to enable my CodeIgniter controller to correctly identify ajax calls from FullCalendar4? A: <p>One of the <a href="https://fullcalendar.io/docs/upgrading-from-v3" rel="nofollow noreferrer">major breaking changes</a> from FullCalendar version 3 to version 4 is</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Removal of jQuery as a dependency.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>This is relevant because consequently:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>X-Requested-With is set only by AJAX libraries like jQuery, Mootools, Prototype etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://forum.codeigniter.com/thread-72688-post-361182.html#pid361182" rel="nofollow noreferrer">according to @InsiteFX</a></li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>CodeIgniter's <code>is_ajax_request()</code> source code looks like this:</p>
<p>/ci/system/core/input.php:</p>
<blockquote>
<pre><code>/**
 * Is AJAX request?
 *
 * Test to see if a request contains the HTTP_X_REQUESTED_WITH header.
 *
 * @return    bool
 */
public function is_ajax_request()
{
  return ( ! empty($_SERVER['HTTP_X_REQUESTED_WITH']) &amp;&amp; strtolower($_SERVER['HTTP_X_REQUESTED_WITH']) === 'xmlhttprequest');
}
</code></pre>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<p>This means a solution can be found a couple of ways...</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Explicitly set the header sought by CodeIgniter (<code>'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest'</code>) in your AJAX call; see <code>events.headers</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>calendar = new FullCalendar.Calendar(calendarEl, {
    // ...
    events: {
         url: '/ci/controller_class/controller_method',
         method: 'POST',
         headers: {
             'Content-Type': 'application/json',
             'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest' /* set header expected by CodeIgniter's is_ajax_request() method */
         },
         extraParams: function () {
             return {
                 action: 'fetch',
                 fpks: $('#userFilter option:selected').map(function () {
                     return this.value;
                 }).get()
             };
         },
         failure: function () {
             showAlert('there was an error while fetching events!');
         },
     },
     // ...
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Hack FullCalendar's /core/main.js file and add:</p>
<p>Add the following line immediately after the <code>xhr.open()</code> call in the <code>requestJson()</code> method:</p>
<pre><code> xhr.setRequestHeader('X-Requested-With', 'xmlhttprequest'); /* Food for CI's $this-&gt;input-&gt;is_ajax_request() */
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>If you are never expecting any $_POST values with a standard page load (this is true of my application), simply do not use <code>is_ajax_request()</code> for this specific task.  In your controller, just use this instead:</p>
<pre><code> if (!empty($_POST)) {
</code></pre>
<p>or more simply (because the superglobal will always be declared)</p>
<pre><code> if ($_POST) {
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>
